<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">North China</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> is the region of China north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a> and the Qinling Mountains, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> basin and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>. Farmed mainly for wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, it was the cradle of early Chinese states and long the seat of government. Its open northern frontier made it the target of steppe peoples, and dynasties of northern origin such as the Northern Wei, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, Jin and Yuan ruled it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-china.jpg" alt="North China" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Quintucket · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:North_China.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To hold the conquered lands, much of the Jurchen population was resettled in <b>North China</b> in military-agricultural communities and given land, some of it taken from Chinese farmers, which caused lasting friction and left many Jurchen households impoverished.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its potters used the wheel and made thin-walled vessels with great skill; the ware spread across <b>North China</b> and is found in the Yangtze valley and as far as the southeastern coast.</blockquote><a href="/culture/longshan-culture">Longshan culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Princes held appanages, grants of land and households whose revenue they drew: as early as 1236 Kublai had received an estate of 10,000 households in <b>North China</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tianjin Daily had no room to print it, but Zhang Heng, editor of the magazine Popular Song Selection, placed it first in his third issue, and the song spread among the workers and students of <b>North China</b>, first in Tianjin, then in Beijing.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ode-to-the-motherland">Ode to the Motherland</a></li><li><blockquote>The shallow magnitude 7.8 earthquake struck Shanxi Province in <b>North China</b> under the Qing dynasty and affected over 120 counties across eight provinces.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-18">18 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Marco Polo Bridge incident of July 1937, the Japanese <b>North China</b> Area Army occupied Beijing and Tianjin and pushed south.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-republic-of-china">Provisional Government of the Republic of China</a></li></ul></section>
